(window.CE_PACK=window.CE_PACK||{})["Operating Systems"]=Object.assign((window.CE_PACK||{})["Operating Systems"]||{},{docs:{"assign":"# Operating Systems: Assignments\n\nThree assignments, seven questions each (21 in all), with complete solutions at the end of each assignment. The C questions should be compiled and run on Linux (gcc, or WSL on Windows); every program and number in the solutions was run or computed. Show your working for scheduling charts and tables.\n\n# Assignment 1: Processes, Threads and CPU Scheduling (Chapters 1 to 4)\n\n**Q1. Modes and system calls.** (a) Explain why user programs cannot execute privileged instructions directly and describe step by step what happens when a program calls `read()` [4]. (b) Give three events that cause a transfer of control to the kernel and say whether each is synchronous or asynchronous [3]. (c) Why does the kernel use a timer interrupt? [3]\n\n**Q2. Reading fork programs.** How many lines does each program print, and what? (a) `fork() && fork(); printf(\"A\\n\");` [2] (b) `for (i = 0; i < 3; i++) if (fork() == 0) break; printf(\"B\\n\");` [3] (c) `int x = 10; if (fork() == 0) { x += 5; printf(\"child x=%d\\n\", x); } else { wait(NULL); printf(\"parent x=%d\\n\", x); }` [3] (d) `fork(); fork(); printf(\"D\\n\");` [2]\n\n**Q3. Threads and data races.** Two threads each execute `for (i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) counter++;` on a shared `long counter`. (a) Why is the final value usually less than 2,000,000? Show an interleaving of the machine-level steps that loses an update [5]. (b) Name two ways to fix it and their costs [3]. (c) What results did the experiment give for the unprotected program and for the mutex and atomic versions? [2]\n\n**Q4. Scheduling comparison.** Processes (arrival, burst): P1 (0, 7), P2 (2, 4), P3 (4, 1), P4 (5, 4). Draw Gantt charts and compute the waiting time of each process and the average for FCFS, non-preemptive SJF, SRTF and round robin with q = 2 [10].\n\n**Q5. Priority scheduling and starvation.** All arrive at time 0: P1 (burst 10, priority 3), P2 (1, 1), P3 (2, 4), P4 (1, 5), P5 (5, 2); a lower number means a higher priority. (a) Non-preemptive priority: Gantt chart and average waiting time [4]. (b) Compare with FCFS, SJF and RR (q = 1) [4]. (c) Explain starvation and how aging fixes it [2].\n\n**Q6. Estimating bursts and context-switch cost.** (a) With tau0 = 10 and alpha = 0.5 and measured bursts 6, 4, 6, 4, compute the exponential-average predictions tau1 to tau4 [4]. (b) A round-robin system has a context switch of 2 ms: what fraction of CPU time is useful for quanta of 10 ms and 50 ms? [3] (c) What is the trade-off in choosing q? [3]\n\n**Q7. Shell pipelines.** (a) Write a C program that runs `printf \"a\\nb\\nc\\n\" | wc -l` using `pipe`, `fork`, `dup2` and `exec` [6]. (b) Why must the parent close both ends of the pipe? [2] (c) What is a zombie and how did we observe one? [2]\n\n## Solutions to Assignment 1\n\n**Q1.** (a) Privileged instructions (I/O, changing page tables, disabling interrupts) trap in user mode, so a user program cannot take over the machine or touch other processes' memory. For `read()`: the C library places the arguments and system-call number in registers and executes a trap instruction; the CPU switches to kernel mode and jumps to the system-call handler; the kernel checks the arguments, finds the file's data (in the page cache or by starting a disk read and blocking the process), copies the bytes into the user buffer, and returns to user mode with the result.\n(b) A system call (synchronous: caused by the instruction being executed), an exception such as a page fault or divide by zero (synchronous), a device or timer interrupt (asynchronous).\n(c) The timer interrupt returns control to the kernel at regular intervals even if the program never makes a system call, so the scheduler can preempt it and no program can monopolise the CPU.\n\n**Q2.** (a) `fork() && fork()`: the original process gets the child's PID (true) and runs the second fork; the first child gets 0 (false) and skips it. The second fork adds one more process: 3 processes in total, so **3 lines** (\"A\" three times). (Verified by running it.)\n(b) Each of the three iterations the parent forks a child that immediately breaks out of the loop and prints; the parent prints after its third fork: 3 children + 1 parent = **4 lines** (\"B\" four times).\n(c) The child prints `child x=15`; the parent waits, then prints `parent x=10` (separate address spaces). **2 lines**, in that order.\n(d) Two forks give 2^2 = **4** processes, each printing \"D\": 4 lines.\n\n**Q3.** (a) `counter++` is load, add, store. Interleaving: T1 loads 100; T2 loads 100; T1 stores 101; T2 stores 101. Two increments, one effect. Over two million increments many are lost.\n(b) A mutex around the increment (correct, but each lock/unlock costs time and serialises the threads); an atomic fetch-and-add instruction (cheaper, still contended). Better still: give each thread a private counter and add them at the end (no sharing).\n(c) Unprotected: **1,976,498**, **1,976,669**, **1,971,753** in three runs (not 2,000,000, and different each time); mutex: 2,000,000 each time; atomic: 2,000,000.\n\n**Q4.**\nFCFS: P1 [0,7], P2 [7,11], P3 [11,12], P4 [12,16]. Waiting: P1 0, P2 5, P3 7, P4 7: **average 4.75**.\nSJF (non-preemptive): P1 [0,7]; at 7 the ready jobs are P2 (4), P3 (1), P4 (4): P3 [7,8], then P2 [8,12], P4 [12,16]. Waiting: P1 0, P2 6, P3 3, P4 7: **average 4.0**.\nSRTF: P1 [0,2]; P2 arrives at 2 with burst 4 against P1's remaining 5, so P2 [2,4]; P3 arrives at 4 with burst 1 against P2's remaining 2, so P3 [4,5]; at 5 the ready processes are P1 (5), P2 (2), P4 (4): P2 [5,7], P4 [7,11], P1 [11,16]. Waiting: P1 9, P2 1, P3 0, P4 2: **average 3.0**.\nRR (q = 2; a newly arrived process joins the queue before the preempted one): P1 [0,2], P2 [2,4], P1 [4,6], P3 [6,7], P2 [7,9], P4 [9,11], P1 [11,13], P4 [13,15], P1 [15,16]. Completion: P3 7, P2 9, P4 15, P1 16. Waiting: P1 9, P2 3, P3 2, P4 6: **average 5.0**.\nSRTF is the best on average waiting time; round robin trades average waiting for better response.\n\n**Q5.** (a) Order by priority: P2, P5, P1, P3, P4. Gantt: P2 [0,1], P5 [1,6], P1 [6,16], P3 [16,18], P4 [18,19]. Waiting: P1 6, P2 0, P3 16, P4 18, P5 1: **average 8.2**.\n(b) FCFS (P1 to P5 in order): completion 10, 11, 13, 14, 19; waiting 0, 10, 11, 13, 14: **9.6**. SJF: order P2, P4, P3, P5, P1: waiting 0, 1, 2, 4, 9: **3.2**. RR with q = 1: completion P2 2, P4 4, P3 7, P5 14, P1 19: waiting P1 9, P2 1, P3 5, P4 3, P5 9: **5.4**.\n(c) With a steady stream of higher-priority arrivals a low-priority process (here P4) may never run: **starvation**. Aging gradually increases the priority of a process while it waits, so eventually it runs.\n\n**Q6.** (a) tau(n+1) = alpha t(n) + (1 - alpha) tau(n): tau1 = 0.5 × 6 + 0.5 × 10 = **8**; tau2 = 0.5 × 4 + 0.5 × 8 = **6**; tau3 = 0.5 × 6 + 0.5 × 6 = **6**; tau4 = 0.5 × 4 + 0.5 × 6 = **5**.\n(b) Useful fraction = q/(q + 2): q = 10 gives 10/12 = **83.3 percent**; q = 50 gives 50/52 = **96.2 percent**.\n(c) A small q gives good response times for interactive jobs but high switching overhead; a large q gives high throughput but poor response and behaviour like FCFS. A good q is longer than most CPU bursts (about 80 percent of them).\n\n**Q7.** (a)\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <sys/wait.h>\n#include <unistd.h>\nint main(void) {\n    int fd[2];\n    pipe(fd);\n    if (fork() == 0) {                 /* writer */\n        dup2(fd[1], STDOUT_FILENO);\n        close(fd[0]); close(fd[1]);\n        execlp(\"printf\", \"printf\", \"a\\\\nb\\\\nc\\\\n\", (char *)NULL);\n        _exit(127);\n    }\n    if (fork() == 0) {                 /* reader */\n        dup2(fd[0], STDIN_FILENO);\n        close(fd[0]); close(fd[1]);\n        execlp(\"wc\", \"wc\", \"-l\", (char *)NULL);\n        _exit(127);\n    }\n    close(fd[0]); close(fd[1]);\n    while (wait(NULL) > 0) ;\n    return 0;\n}\n```\nIt printed **3**.\n(b) `wc` reads until end-of-file, which occurs only when **every** write end of the pipe is closed. If the parent kept its copy of fd[1] open, `wc` would wait forever. Closing the read end in the parent and the unused ends in the children also releases descriptors.\n(c) A zombie is a terminated child whose parent has not yet called `wait`: the kernel keeps its exit status and PCB entry. In the experiment the child exited, the parent slept one second and `/proc/<pid>/stat` showed state **Z**; after `waitpid` the entry was gone.\n\n# Assignment 2: Synchronisation and Deadlocks (Chapter 5)\n\n**Q1. Peterson's algorithm.** (a) Explain why each of mutual exclusion, progress and bounded waiting holds [4]. (b) Suppose the two lines of lock(i) are swapped (`turn = j` first, then `flag[i] = 1`). Show an interleaving in which both threads enter the critical section [4]. (c) Why does a naive C implementation with ordinary variables fail on modern hardware? [2]\n\n**Q2. Semaphore ordering.** A bounded-buffer producer executes `lock(mu); wait(empty); put(item); signal(full); unlock(mu);` and the consumer `lock(mu); wait(full); item = get(); signal(empty); unlock(mu);`. (a) Show a deadlock scenario [4]. (b) Give the correct order [3]. (c) Why do producer-consumer solutions need three synchronisation objects (two semaphores and a mutex)? [3]\n\n**Q3. Condition variables.** (a) Write a bounded buffer (capacity 4) using one mutex and two condition variables [6]. (b) Why must the wait be inside a `while` loop, not an `if`? [2] (c) Why does `pthread_cond_wait` take the mutex as an argument? [2]\n\n**Q4. Dining philosophers.** (a) Show how the \"pick up left then right\" protocol deadlocks [3]. (b) Give two deadlock-free solutions and argue why each works [4]. (c) Which Coffman condition does each break? [3]\n\n**Q5. Deadlock detection.** Five processes, three resource types, Available = (0, 0, 0). Allocation: P0 (0,1,0), P1 (2,0,0), P2 (3,0,3), P3 (2,1,1), P4 (0,0,2). Requests: P0 (0,0,0), P1 (2,0,2), P2 (0,0,0), P3 (1,0,0), P4 (0,0,2). (a) Is the system deadlocked? [5] (b) If P2 now requests (0,0,1), what is the answer, and which processes are deadlocked? [5]\n\n**Q6. Banker's algorithm.** Allocation: P0 (0,1,0), P1 (2,0,0), P2 (3,0,2), P3 (2,1,1), P4 (0,0,2); Max: P0 (7,5,3), P1 (3,2,2), P2 (9,0,2), P3 (2,2,2), P4 (4,3,3); Available (3,3,2). (a) Compute Need and find a safe sequence [4]. (b) P1 requests (1,0,2): grant? [2] (c) Then P4 requests (3,3,0): grant? [2] (d) Then P0 requests (0,2,0): grant? [2]\n\n**Q7. Lock ordering in practice.** A bank function `transfer(a, b, amount)` locks `a.lock`, then `b.lock`. (a) Show how concurrent transfer(x, y) and transfer(y, x) deadlock [3]. (b) Fix it by ordering locks [3]. (c) Describe the experiment that demonstrated this and its outcome [2]. (d) Name two other strategies (trylock with backoff, a single global lock) and their drawbacks [2].\n\n## Solutions to Assignment 2\n\n**Q1.** (a) Step 1: Mutual exclusion: thread i enters only if flag[j] = 0 or turn = i.\nStep 2: If both are in, both flags are 1, so each saw turn equal to its own number; but turn is written last by one of them (turn = j by thread i, or turn = i by thread j), so it cannot equal both.\nStep 3: Progress: a thread outside the entry code has flag = 0 so it never blocks the other.\nStep 4: Bounded waiting: if i waits while j is repeatedly entering, j sets turn = i when it wants in again, so i enters after at most one entry by j.\n(b) Swapped: T0: turn = 1. T1: turn = 0. T1: flag[1] = 1. T1: tests flag[0] (still 0) and enters. T0: flag[0] = 1, tests flag[1] = 1 and turn == 1? turn is 0, so the wait condition is false and T0 enters too. **Both are inside the critical section.**\n(c) The compiler and CPU may reorder the store to flag and the load of the other flag (store buffers: a store may not be visible to the other core before the later load executes), so both threads can see the other's flag as 0. Use sequentially consistent atomics (C11 `atomic_int`) or memory fences; the tested C11 version gave the exact count of 2,000,000.\n\n**Q2.** (a) The buffer is full; a producer takes `mu` and blocks in `wait(empty)` while holding `mu`; the consumer needs `mu` to remove an item and call `signal(empty)`, but cannot get it: **deadlock**.\n(b) Wait on the semaphore first, then lock: `wait(empty); lock(mu); put; unlock(mu); signal(full);` and `wait(full); lock(mu); get; unlock(mu); signal(empty);`.\n(c) `empty` blocks producers when the buffer is full, `full` blocks consumers when it is empty; the mutex protects the buffer indices from concurrent modification by several producers or consumers. Semaphores count slots but do not by themselves make `put`/`get` atomic.\n\n**Q3.** (a) (Tested: 100,000 items transferred, produced sum = consumed sum = 5,000,050,000.)\n```c\nint buf[4], in = 0, out = 0, count = 0;\npthread_mutex_t m = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;\npthread_cond_t not_full = PTHREAD_COND_INITIALIZER, not_empty = PTHREAD_COND_INITIALIZER;\nvoid put(int v) {\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&m);\n    while (count == 4) pthread_cond_wait(&not_full, &m);\n    buf[in] = v; in = (in + 1) % 4; count++;\n    pthread_cond_signal(&not_empty);\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&m);\n}\nint get(void) {\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&m);\n    while (count == 0) pthread_cond_wait(&not_empty, &m);\n    int v = buf[out]; out = (out + 1) % 4; count--;\n    pthread_cond_signal(&not_full);\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&m);\n    return v;\n}\n```\n(b) After a wakeup the condition may be false again: another consumer may have taken the item first, or the wakeup may be spurious. The loop re-tests the condition (Mesa semantics).\n(c) Checking the condition and sleeping must be atomic: if the mutex were not released atomically with going to sleep, a signal sent between the check and the sleep would be lost. `pthread_cond_wait` atomically releases the mutex, sleeps, and re-acquires the mutex before returning.\n\n**Q4.** (a) All five philosophers pick up their left fork at the same time. Each now holds one fork and waits for the right fork held by the neighbour: circular wait, deadlock.\n(b) (1) **Resource ordering**: number the forks and always take the lower-numbered fork first; the philosopher with forks 4 and 0 takes 0 first, so a cycle is impossible. (Tested in C: five threads, 20,000 meals each, all finished with 100,000 meals.) (2) **Limit to four philosophers** at the table (a counting semaphore initialised to 4): at least one can always get both forks. Another: take both forks atomically under one mutex.\n(c) Resource ordering breaks **circular wait**; limiting to four prevents the cycle (also circular wait); taking both atomically breaks **hold and wait**.\n\n**Q5.** Step 1: Detection uses Requests: Work = (0,0,0).\nStep 2: P0 holds (0,1,0) and requests nothing: finishes, Work = (0,1,0).\nStep 3: P2 requests nothing: Work = (3,1,3).\nStep 4: P3 requests (1,0,0) <= Work: Work = (5,2,4).\nStep 5: P4 requests (0,0,2) <= Work: Work = (5,2,6).\nStep 6: P1 requests (2,0,2) <= Work: Work = (7,2,6).\nStep 7: All finish: **not deadlocked** (order <P0, P2, P3, P4, P1>).\n(b) With P2 requesting (0,0,1): P0 finishes, giving Work = (0,1,0). No other process can have its request met: P1 needs (2,0,2), P2 (0,0,1), P3 (1,0,0), P4 (0,0,2). So **P1, P2, P3, P4 are deadlocked** (P0 is not, it holds resources but is not waiting).\n\n**Q6.** (a) Need = Max - Allocation: P0 (7,4,3), P1 (1,2,2), P2 (6,0,0), P3 (0,1,1), P4 (4,3,1). Safe sequence **<P1, P3, P4, P0, P2>**: Work (3,3,2) → P1 → (5,3,2) → P3 → (7,4,3) → P4 → (7,4,5) → P0 → (7,5,5) → P2 → (10,5,7).\n(b) (1,0,2) <= Need(P1) and <= Available: tentatively Available = (2,3,0), Allocation(P1) = (3,0,2), Need(P1) = (0,2,0). The safety algorithm finds <P1, P3, P4, P0, P2>: **safe, grant**.\n(c) P4 asks for (3,3,0) but Available is only (2,3,0): **must wait** (not available).\n(d) P0 asks for (0,2,0) <= Available: tentatively Available = (2,1,0), Need(P0) = (7,2,3). With Available = (2,1,0) no process can finish: P1 needs (0,2,0) (two of the second resource, only 1 free), P2 needs (6,0,0), P3 needs (0,1,1) (the third resource is exhausted), P4 needs (4,3,1) and P0 now needs (7,2,3): **unsafe, do not grant**; P0 waits. (From the original state the same request would have been safe: safety depends on the current state.)\n\n**Q7.** (a) T1 runs transfer(x, y): locks x then waits for y. T2 runs transfer(y, x): locks y then waits for x: circular wait.\n(b) Always lock in a global order, for example by account number:\n```c\nvoid transfer(account *a, account *b, long amt) {\n    account *first = a->id < b->id ? a : b, *second = a->id < b->id ? b : a;\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&first->lock);\n    pthread_mutex_lock(&second->lock);\n    a->balance -= amt; b->balance += amt;\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&second->lock);\n    pthread_mutex_unlock(&first->lock);\n}\n```\n(c) Two threads took locks A and B in opposite orders; each used a timed lock of one second: both reported a timeout (deadlock). With a consistent order both got both locks.\n(d) `trylock` with backoff and retry avoids deadlock but may livelock and wastes work; a single global lock is simple and safe but serialises all transfers (no parallelism).\n\n# Assignment 3: Memory, Storage and File Systems (Chapters 6 to 8)\n\n**Q1. Address translation.** A 16-bit virtual address space with 1 KB pages. (a) How many pages and offset bits? [2] (b) Translate virtual address 0xB5A3 if page 45 is in frame 7 [3] (c) What happens for an address in a page with the valid bit clear? [3] (d) Explain the role of the dirty and reference bits [2]\n\n**Q2. Page table sizes.** (a) 32-bit addresses, 4 KB pages, 4-byte entries: the size of a flat page table [2]. (b) A two-level table with a 10 + 10 + 12 split: the memory used by a process that uses only the lowest 8 MB and the top 4 MB of its address space [4]. (c) Why does x86-64 use four levels of 9 bits? [2] (d) What is internal fragmentation for a 72,766-byte program with 2048-byte pages? [2]\n\n**Q3. Effective access time.** (a) TLB lookup 5 ns, memory access 50 ns, hit ratio 95 percent: EAT [3]. (b) Memory access 200 ns, page-fault service 8 ms: EAT for p = 1/1000 and the largest p for a 10 percent slowdown [4]. (c) Explain why locality makes (b) tolerable in practice [3].\n\n**Q4. Page replacement.** (a) Reference string 1, 2, 3, 2, 4, 1, 5, 2, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 4: FIFO, LRU and OPT with 3 and 4 frames [5]. (b) Reference string 2, 3, 2, 1, 5, 2, 4, 5, 3, 2, 5, 2: the same [5].\n\n**Q5. Contiguous allocation.** Partitions (KB) in order: 300, 600, 350, 200, 750, 125. Requests in order: 115, 500, 358, 200, 375. Apply first fit, best fit and worst fit; which requests fail, and what is left? [10]\n\n**Q6. Disk scheduling.** Cylinders 0 to 99, head at 50 (moving up), queue 45, 21, 67, 90, 4, 50, 89, 52, 61, 87, 25. Total head movement for FCFS, SSTF, LOOK (up first), SCAN (up first) and C-SCAN [10].\n\n**Q7. File systems.** (a) With 1 KB blocks, 4-byte pointers and an inode with 12 direct, one single, one double and one triple indirect pointer: the maximum file size and the pointers needed for a 100 MB file [5]. (b) Hard link versus symbolic link: describe the experiment (link count, deleting the original) [3]. (c) What does journaling protect against? [2]\n\n## Solutions to Assignment 3\n\n**Q1.** (a) 2^16 bytes / 2^10 = 64 pages, offset 10 bits (page number 6 bits).\n(b) Step 1: 0xB5A3 = 1011 0101 1010 0011 in binary.\nStep 2: The top 6 bits 101101 give page **45**; the offset is the low 10 bits 01 1010 0011 = 0x1A3 = **419**.\nStep 3: Frame 7: physical address = 7 × 1024 + 419 = 7587 = **0x1DA3**.\n(c) The MMU raises a page fault trap: the kernel checks whether the access is legal; if so it brings the page in (finding a free frame or evicting one) and restarts the instruction; if the address is illegal the process gets SIGSEGV.\n(d) The reference bit is set by hardware on any access (used by clock/LRU approximations); the dirty bit is set on a write: a clean victim page can simply be discarded, a dirty one must be written back first.\n\n**Q2.** (a) 2^20 pages × 4 B = **4 MB** per process.\n(b) Each second-level table maps 1024 pages × 4 KB = 4 MB. The lowest 8 MB needs 2 second-level tables and the top 4 MB needs 1 more, plus the single top-level table: 4 tables × 4 KB = **16 KB**, instead of the 4 MB of a flat table.\n(c) Each table holds 512 eight-byte entries = 4096 bytes, exactly one page; 4 levels × 9 bits + 12 offset bits = 48 bits of virtual address.\n(d) 72,766 mod 2048 = 1086, so the last page is 1086/2048 used: waste = 2048 - 1086 = **962 bytes**; the program needs 36 pages.\n\n**Q3.** (a) EAT = 0.95 (5 + 50) + 0.05 (5 + 100) = 52.25 + 5.25 = **57.5 ns**.\n(b) EAT = (1 - 0.001) × 200 + 0.001 × 8,000,000 = 199.8 + 8000 = **8199.8 ns** (a 41 times slowdown). For EAT <= 220: p <= 20/(8,000,000 - 200) = **2.5 × 10^-6** (one fault per 400,000 accesses).\n(c) Programs reference a small working set repeatedly (temporal locality) and nearby addresses (spatial locality), so after the working set is loaded faults are extremely rare; the fault rate is far below 1 in 400,000 in a well-behaved program.\n\n**Q4.** (a) Reference string 1, 2, 3, 2, 4, 1, 5, 2, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 4. Trace for 3 frames (frames listed oldest first for FIFO, least recently used first for LRU):\nFIFO: 1: [1] fault; 2: [1, 2] fault; 3: [1, 2, 3] fault; 2: [1, 2, 3] hit; 4: [2, 3, 4] fault; 1: [3, 4, 1] fault; 5: [4, 1, 5] fault; 2: [1, 5, 2] fault; 1: [1, 5, 2] hit; 2: [1, 5, 2] hit; 3: [5, 2, 3] fault; 4: [2, 3, 4] fault; 5: [3, 4, 5] fault; 4: [3, 4, 5] hit. Total **10 faults**.\nLRU: 1: [1] fault; 2: [1, 2] fault; 3: [1, 2, 3] fault; 2: [1, 3, 2] hit; 4: [3, 2, 4] fault; 1: [2, 4, 1] fault; 5: [4, 1, 5] fault; 2: [1, 5, 2] fault; 1: [5, 2, 1] hit; 2: [5, 1, 2] hit; 3: [1, 2, 3] fault; 4: [2, 3, 4] fault; 5: [3, 4, 5] fault; 4: [3, 5, 4] hit. Total **10 faults**.\nOPT: 1: [1] fault; 2: [1, 2] fault; 3: [1, 2, 3] fault; 2: [1, 2, 3] hit; 4: [1, 2, 4] fault; 1: [1, 2, 4] hit; 5: [1, 2, 5] fault; 2: [1, 2, 5] hit; 1: [1, 2, 5] hit; 2: [1, 2, 5] hit; 3: [2, 5, 3] fault; 4: [5, 3, 4] fault; 5: [5, 3, 4] hit; 4: [5, 3, 4] hit. Total **7 faults**.\nWith 4 frames the same method gives: FIFO **10**, LRU **8**, OPT **6** faults (OPT evicts the page whose next use is farthest away).\nSummary (a): 3 frames: FIFO **10**, LRU **10**, OPT **7**. 4 frames: FIFO **10**, LRU **8**, OPT **6**.\n(b) Reference string 2, 3, 2, 1, 5, 2, 4, 5, 3, 2, 5, 2. Trace for 3 frames:\nFIFO: 2: [2] fault; 3: [2, 3] fault; 2: [2, 3] hit; 1: [2, 3, 1] fault; 5: [3, 1, 5] fault; 2: [1, 5, 2] fault; 4: [5, 2, 4] fault; 5: [5, 2, 4] hit; 3: [2, 4, 3] fault; 2: [2, 4, 3] hit; 5: [4, 3, 5] fault; 2: [3, 5, 2] fault. Total **9 faults**.\nLRU: 2: [2] fault; 3: [2, 3] fault; 2: [3, 2] hit; 1: [3, 2, 1] fault; 5: [2, 1, 5] fault; 2: [1, 5, 2] hit; 4: [5, 2, 4] fault; 5: [2, 4, 5] hit; 3: [4, 5, 3] fault; 2: [5, 3, 2] fault; 5: [3, 2, 5] hit; 2: [3, 5, 2] hit. Total **7 faults**.\nOPT: 2: [2] fault; 3: [2, 3] fault; 2: [2, 3] hit; 1: [2, 3, 1] fault; 5: [2, 3, 5] fault; 2: [2, 3, 5] hit; 4: [3, 5, 4] fault; 5: [3, 5, 4] hit; 3: [3, 5, 4] hit; 2: [5, 4, 2] fault; 5: [5, 4, 2] hit; 2: [5, 4, 2] hit. Total **6 faults**.\nSummary (b): 3 frames: FIFO **9**, LRU **7**, OPT **6**. 4 frames: FIFO **6**, LRU **6**, OPT **5**.\nIn both cases OPT is a lower bound and LRU is at least as good as FIFO here.\n\n**Q5.**\nFirst fit: 115 goes to 300 (185 left), 500 goes to 600 (100 left), 358 goes to 750 (392 left), 200 goes to 350 (150 left), 375 goes to 392: **all placed**; left: 185, 100, 150, 200, 17, 125.\nBest fit: 115 goes to 125 (10 left), 500 goes to 600 (100), 358 goes to 750 (392), 200 goes to 200 (0), 375 goes to 392 (17): **all placed**; left: 300, 100, 350, 0, 17, 10.\nWorst fit: 115 goes to 750 (635), 500 goes to 635 (135), 358 goes to 600 (242), 200 goes to 350 (150), 375: the largest remaining is 300: **fails**. Left: 300, 242, 150, 200, 135, 125.\nWorst fit fails because it keeps splitting the largest block; best fit leaves tiny holes (10 and 17 KB) of little use but placed everything here.\n\n**Q6.** Head at 50 moving up.\nFCFS: 50→45→21→67→90→4→50→89→52→61→87→25: |5| + 24 + 46 + 23 + 86 + 46 + 39 + 37 + 9 + 26 + 62 = **403**.\nSSTF: 50 (0), 52, 45, 61, 67, 87, 89, 90, 25, 21, 4: **140**.\nLOOK (up first): 50, 52, 61, 67, 87, 89, 90, then back 45, 25, 21, 4: (90 - 50) + (90 - 4) = 40 + 86 = **126**.\nSCAN (up first, to the last cylinder 99, then back to 4): (99 - 50) + (99 - 4) = 49 + 95 = **144**.\nC-SCAN (up to 99, jump to 0, then up to 45): 49 + 99 + 45 = **193**. (C-LOOK, going up to 90 then jumping to 4 and up to 45, would give 167.)\n\n**Q7.** (a) Step 1: 1 KB blocks with 4-byte pointers: 256 pointers per block.\nStep 2: Capacity in blocks: 12 + 256 + 256^2 + 256^3 = 12 + 256 + 65,536 + 16,777,216 = 16,843,020 blocks, i.e. **about 16.06 GiB**.\nStep 3: A 100 MB file = 102,400 blocks: 12 direct, 256 through the single indirect block, 65,536 through the double indirect, and the remaining 102,400 - 12 - 256 - 65,536 = **36,596** blocks through the **triple indirect** pointer (which needs 1 triple-indirect block, 1 second-level block and ceil(36,596/256) = 143 blocks of data pointers).\nStep 4: So a 100 MB file does use the triple indirect pointer with 1 KB blocks.\n(b) Step 1: A hard link is a second directory entry pointing to the same inode: after `ln f hard`, both showed `links=2`, inode 29.\nStep 2: After `rm f` the data was still readable through `hard` (links dropped to 1).\nStep 3: A symbolic link is a separate small file (inode 30, size 1 = the length of the path \"f\") holding a path: after `rm f` the link dangled: `cat soft` failed with \"No such file or directory\".\n(c) Journaling protects metadata consistency after a crash or power failure: intended changes are written to a log first, so recovery replays or discards complete transactions in seconds rather than scanning the whole disk with fsck.\n","exam":"# Operating Systems: Final Examination\n\nDuration: 3 hours. Calculator and a one-page formula sheet allowed. Total: 100 marks.\n- Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions, 1 mark each (20 marks). Answer all.\n- Section B: 8 short questions, 5 marks each (40 marks). Answer all.\n- Section C: 5 long questions, 10 marks each. Answer any four (40 marks).\n\n## Section A: Multiple Choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** A system call causes the CPU to\nA) stay in user mode  B) switch to kernel mode through a trap  C) reboot  D) flush the TLB only\n\n**2.** After `fork()` in Linux, parent and child initially\nA) share all memory writable  B) have separate address spaces (copy-on-write)  C) share the stack only  D) have no open files\n\n**3.** `exec()` does what?\nA) creates a new process  B) replaces the current program image  C) waits for a child  D) kills the parent\n\n**4.** A pipe's reader sees end-of-file only when\nA) the writer sleeps  B) the buffer is empty  C) all write ends are closed  D) the reader closes its end\n\n**5.** Which signal cannot be caught or ignored?\nA) SIGINT  B) SIGUSR1  C) SIGCHLD  D) SIGKILL\n\n**6.** The effect of a very large round-robin quantum is that RR behaves like\nA) SJF  B) FCFS  C) priority scheduling  D) SRTF\n\n**7.** SRTF is\nA) preemptive SJF  B) non-preemptive FCFS  C) round robin  D) a real-time algorithm\n\n**8.** In a multilevel feedback queue, a process that uses its entire quantum is typically\nA) moved up to a higher priority  B) moved down to a lower priority  C) terminated  D) swapped out\n\n**9.** A race condition exists when\nA) two threads run on different CPUs  B) the result depends on the relative timing of unsynchronised accesses to shared data  C) a thread sleeps  D) a mutex is locked twice\n\n**10.** A counting semaphore initialised to N is used to\nA) provide mutual exclusion only  B) count N identical resources  C) measure time  D) wake all threads\n\n**11.** In the producer-consumer solution with semaphores, calling `lock(mutex)` before `wait(empty)` can cause\nA) starvation only  B) deadlock  C) a race  D) no problem\n\n**12.** Dining philosophers with each philosopher picking up the left fork first can\nA) deadlock  B) never deadlock  C) livelock only  D) starve only the first philosopher\n\n**13.** Breaking the circular-wait condition can be done by\nA) allowing preemption  B) imposing a total order on resources  C) allocating all resources at once  D) using more memory\n\n**14.** In a resource-allocation graph with multiple instances per resource, a cycle\nA) always means deadlock  B) is necessary but not sufficient for deadlock  C) never means deadlock  D) means a safe state\n\n**15.** The page size is 2 KB and the address 5000 lies in page number\nA) 1  B) 2  C) 3  D) 5\n\n**16.** A multilevel page table saves memory because it\nA) compresses pages  B) allocates tables only for the parts of the address space in use  C) removes the TLB  D) uses larger pages\n\n**17.** A page fault occurs when\nA) the TLB hits  B) a referenced page is not in memory (valid bit clear)  C) the disk fails  D) a process ends\n\n**18.** Which page replacement algorithm is optimal but unimplementable?\nA) FIFO  B) LRU  C) OPT  D) Clock\n\n**19.** In RAID 5 with n disks, usable capacity is\nA) n  B) n - 1  C) n / 2  D) n - 2\n\n**20.** A hard link\nA) is a file containing a path name  B) is a second directory entry referring to the same inode  C) cannot be deleted  D) always crosses file systems\n\n## Section B: Short Questions (40 marks)\n\n**B1. (5)** How many processes exist after the loop `for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) fork();` has been run by one initial process (count all processes, including the original)? Explain.\n\n**B2. (5)** Two threads each execute `if (balance >= 80) balance -= 80;` with `balance = 100`, with no synchronisation. What final values are possible? Show an interleaving for each and fix the code.\n\n**B3. (5)** Use two semaphores to make two threads print ABABAB... in strict alternation (T1 prints A, T2 prints B). Give the initial values and the code of each thread.\n\n**B4. (5)** For each technique name the Coffman condition it breaks: (a) a process must request all its resources before it starts; (b) resources are numbered and requested in increasing order; (c) if a request cannot be granted the process releases everything it holds and retries; (d) mutual exclusion cannot be broken for a printer: why?\n\n**B5. (5)** A system has 32-bit virtual addresses and 16 KB pages. (a) Give the number of offset bits, the number of pages and the size of a flat page table with 4-byte entries [3]. (b) Translate the address 0x00C0FFEE into its page number and offset [2].\n\n**B6. (5)** TLB access 10 ns, memory access 80 ns, TLB hit ratio 96 percent. Find the effective access time.\n\n**B7. (5)** A 5400 rpm disk has an average seek of 12 ms and a transfer rate of 100 MB/s. Find the average time to read one 8 KB block at a random location.\n\n**B8. (5)** An inode has 12 direct pointers, one single and one double indirect pointer. Blocks are 2 KB and pointers 4 bytes. Find the maximum file size.\n\n## Section C: Long Questions (answer any four, 10 marks each)\n\n**C1. CPU scheduling.** Processes (arrival, burst): P1 (0, 6), P2 (1, 8), P3 (2, 7), P4 (3, 3).\n(a) FCFS: Gantt chart and average waiting time [2]. (b) Non-preemptive SJF [2]. (c) SRTF [2]. (d) Round robin, q = 4 (a process arriving when another is preempted joins the queue first) [3]. (e) Which algorithm is best for average waiting time and what is its drawback? [1]\n\n**C2. Synchronisation.** (a) Write the bounded-buffer producer and consumer using two counting semaphores and a mutex, with correct ordering [4]. (b) Show the deadlock that arises if the mutex is taken first [2]. (c) Describe the experiment on an unprotected shared counter (two threads, 1,000,000 increments each) and the results of the three runs and the protected versions [2]. (d) Explain how resource ordering solves dining philosophers [2].\n\n**C3. Deadlock.** Five processes, three resource types. Available = (2, 1, 1). Allocation: P0 (2,0,1), P1 (1,1,0), P2 (1,0,2), P3 (0,1,1), P4 (1,2,0). Max: P0 (4,2,2), P1 (3,2,1), P2 (3,1,3), P3 (1,3,2), P4 (3,3,1).\n(a) Compute Need [2]. (b) Is the state safe? Give a safe sequence [4]. (c) P1 requests (1,0,1): can it be granted? [3] (d) P3 requests (2,1,0): what is wrong? [1]\n\n**C4. Virtual memory.** Reference string 1, 2, 3, 4, 2, 1, 5, 6, 2, 1, 2, 3, 7, 6, 3, 2, 1, 2, 3, 6.\n(a) Give the fault counts for FIFO, LRU and OPT with 3 frames and show the FIFO trace [5]. (b) The counts with 4 and 5 frames [3]. (c) Define thrashing and the working-set model [2].\n\n**C5. Storage and file systems.** Disk cylinders 0 to 99, head at 50 moving up, queue 45, 21, 67, 90, 4, 50, 89, 52, 61, 87, 25.\n(a) Total head movement for FCFS, SSTF, LOOK, SCAN and C-SCAN [5]. (b) Compare RAID 0, 1, 5 and 10 for four 1 TB disks (capacity and failures tolerated) [3]. (c) Explain how journaling gives crash consistency [2].\n\n---\n\n# Marking Scheme and Complete Solutions\n\n## Section A answers\n1 **B** · 2 **B** · 3 **B** · 4 **C** · 5 **D** · 6 **B** · 7 **A** · 8 **B** · 9 **B** · 10 **B** · 11 **B** · 12 **A** · 13 **B** · 14 **B** · 15 **B** · 16 **B** · 17 **B** · 18 **C** · 19 **B** · 20 **B**\n\nBrief reasons: 4. The write end may also be held open by another process, e.g. the parent, which is why it must close it. 11. A producer holding the mutex can wait for space that only a consumer (needing the mutex) can create. 15. 5000/2048 = 2.44, so page 2. 19. One disk's worth of parity.\n\n## Section B solutions\n\n**B1.** Each fork doubles the number of running processes: 2^4 = **16** processes (the original and 15 new ones). Process creation: iteration i is executed by every process that exists then.\n\n**B2.** Step 1: Possible final values: **20** (serial: the first thread subtracts, the second sees 20 and does nothing) and **-60** (both threads pass the test while balance is 100, then both subtract).\nStep 2: Interleaving for -60: T1 tests 100 >= 80 (true); T2 tests 100 >= 80 (true); T1 subtracts (20); T2 subtracts (-60).\nStep 3: The check and the update must be one atomic step: wrap them in `lock(m); if (balance >= 80) balance -= 80; unlock(m);`.\n\n**B3.** s1 = 1, s2 = 0. T1: loop { `wait(s1)`; print A; `signal(s2)` }. T2: loop { `wait(s2)`; print B; `signal(s1)` }. T1 can print first, then must wait for T2 to give back s1, and so on: ABABAB.\n\n**B4.** (a) Hold and wait (the process never holds some resources while waiting for others). (b) Circular wait. (c) No preemption (the resources are effectively taken away from the waiting process). (d) A printer cannot be shared safely (interleaved output would be garbage), so mutual exclusion is intrinsic; spooling (a daemon owns the printer and queues jobs) removes the need for processes to hold it.\n\n**B5.** (a) Step 1: 16 KB = 2^14: **14 offset bits**.\nStep 2: page-number bits = 32 - 14 = 18, so **2^18 = 262,144 pages**.\nStep 3: flat table = 262,144 × 4 B = **1 MB**.\n(b) Step 1: 0x00C0FFEE = 12,648,430. Page number = 12,648,430 >> 14 = 12,648,430/16,384 = 771.99, so **771 = 0x303**.\nStep 2: offset = 12,648,430 - 771 × 16,384 = 12,648,430 - 12,632,064 = **16,366 = 0x3FEE**.\n\n**B6.** Step 1: TLB hit (96 percent): 10 + 80 = 90 ns. TLB miss (4 percent): 10 + 80 (page table) + 80 (data) = 170 ns.\nStep 2: EAT = 0.96 × 90 + 0.04 × 170 = 86.4 + 6.8 = **93.2 ns**.\n\n**B7.** Step 1: average rotational latency = half a rotation: 60,000 ms/5400/2 = 11.11/2 = **5.56 ms**.\nStep 2: transfer of 8 KB at 100 MB/s = 8/(100 × 1024) s = 0.078 ms.\nStep 3: total = 12 (seek) + 5.56 + 0.08 = **17.6 ms**.\n\n**B8.** Step 1: 2 KB blocks with 4-byte pointers hold 2048/4 = 512 pointers.\nStep 2: maximum blocks = 12 + 512 + 512² = 12 + 512 + 262,144 = 262,668.\nStep 3: size = 262,668 × 2 KB = 525,336 KB = **about 513 MB** (513.02 MiB).\n\n## Section C solutions\n\n**C1.**\n(a) FCFS: P1 [0,6], P2 [6,14], P3 [14,21], P4 [21,24]. Waiting: P1 0, P2 5, P3 12, P4 18: **average 8.75**.\n(b) SJF: P1 [0,6]; at 6 the ready jobs are P2 (8), P3 (7), P4 (3): P4 [6,9], P3 [9,16], P2 [16,24]. Waiting: P1 0, P2 15, P3 7, P4 3: **average 6.25**.\n(c) SRTF: no arrival has a shorter remaining time than the running job: P1 has 6 - 3 = 3 remaining when P4 (3) arrives at t = 3 (a tie, keep running P1); the schedule is the same as (b): **average 6.25**.\n(d) RR q = 4: P1 [0,4], P2 [4,8], P3 [8,12], P4 [12,15], P1 [15,17], P2 [17,21], P3 [21,24]. Completion: P4 15, P1 17, P2 21, P3 24. Waiting: P1 11, P2 12, P3 15, P4 9: **average 11.75**.\n(e) SJF/SRTF minimise average waiting time but need the burst length in advance and can starve long jobs. RR is fair with good response but a higher average wait.\n\n**C2.**\n(a)\n```\nsem empty = N, full = 0;  mutex mu;\nproducer: wait(empty); lock(mu); put(item); unlock(mu); signal(full);\nconsumer: wait(full);  lock(mu); item = get(); unlock(mu); signal(empty);\n```\nTested in C with an 8-slot buffer and 100,000 items: produced sum = consumed sum = 5,000,050,000.\n(b) With the mutex taken first: the buffer is full, a producer locks `mu` and blocks in `wait(empty)`; a consumer cannot lock `mu` to remove an item, so `empty` is never signalled: deadlock.\n(c) Two threads each executed `counter++` one million times. Unprotected: **1,976,498, 1,976,669 and 1,971,753** (varying, always less than 2,000,000); with a mutex and with an atomic add: **2,000,000** every time.\n(d) Number the forks 0 to 4 and let each philosopher take the lower-numbered fork first. Philosopher 4 (forks 4 and 0) takes fork 0 first. A cycle of waiting would need every philosopher to hold a lower fork and wait for a higher one, but the philosopher holding fork 4 would wait for a lower-numbered fork, contradicting the ordering: no circular wait is possible. Tested in C (five threads, 20,000 meals each): all finished.\n\n**C3.**\n(a) Need = Max - Allocation: P0 (2,2,1), P1 (2,1,1), P2 (2,1,1), P3 (1,2,1), P4 (2,1,1).\n(b) Step 1: Work = (2,1,1).\nStep 2: P0 needs (2,2,1): 2 > 1 in the second resource, no.\nStep 3: P1 needs (2,1,1) <= Work: yes; Work = (2,1,1) + (1,1,0) = (3,2,1).\nStep 4: P2 (2,1,1) <= (3,2,1): yes; Work = (4,2,3).\nStep 5: P3 (1,2,1): yes; Work = (4,3,4).\nStep 6: P4 (2,1,1): yes; Work = (5,5,4).\nStep 7: P0 (2,2,1): yes; Work = (7,5,5).\nStep 8: **Safe**, sequence **<P1, P2, P3, P4, P0>**.\n(c) Step 1: (1,0,1) <= Need(P1) = (2,1,1) and <= Available (2,1,1): tentatively Available = (1,1,0), Allocation(P1) = (2,1,1), Need(P1) = (1,1,0).\nStep 2: Safety: P1 (1,1,0) <= (1,1,0): finishes, Work = (3,2,1) (it releases its allocation (2,1,1)); then P2 (2,1,1): Work = (4,2,3); P3: (5,3,4); P4: (6,5,4); P0: (8,5,5).\nStep 3: Safe: **grant**.\n(d) The request (2,1,0) exceeds P3's remaining need (1,2,1) in the first resource (2 > 1): the process has **exceeded its declared maximum claim**: an error, not just a wait.\n\n**C4.**\n(a) 3 frames: FIFO **16**, LRU **15**, OPT **11**. FIFO trace (F = fault, h = hit): 1F 2F 3F 4F 2h 1F 5F 6F 2F 1F 2h 3F 7F 6F 3h 2F 1F 2h 3F 6F. Frame contents after each fault: [1], [1,2], [1,2,3], [2,3,4], hit, [3,4,1], [4,1,5], [1,5,6], [5,6,2], [6,2,1], hit, [2,1,3], [1,3,7], [3,7,6], hit, [7,6,2], [6,2,1], hit, [2,1,3], [1,3,6]: 16 faults, 4 hits.\n(b) 4 frames: FIFO **14**, LRU **10**, OPT **8**. 5 frames: FIFO **10**, LRU **8**, OPT **7**. (More frames never hurt on this string.)\n(c) **Thrashing**: a process (or the system) has too few frames for its working set, so it faults continuously and the CPU idles while the paging disk is saturated. The **working set** W(t, D) is the set of pages referenced in the last D references; the OS allocates each process at least |W| frames and suspends processes if the sum of working sets exceeds memory.\n\n**C5.**\n(a) FCFS **403**; SSTF **140** (50, 52, 45, 61, 67, 87, 89, 90, 25, 21, 4); LOOK (up first) **126** (40 up to 90, then 86 down to 4); SCAN (up to the end 99, then down to 4) **144**; C-SCAN (up to 99, jump to 0, up to 45) **193**. (C-LOOK gives 167.)\n(b) RAID 0: 4 TB, tolerates no failure. RAID 1 (two mirrored pairs, or 4-way mirror): 2 TB (or 1 TB for 4 copies), tolerates 1 failure per pair. RAID 5: 3 TB, tolerates **1** failure. RAID 10: 2 TB, tolerates 1 failure per mirror pair (up to 2 if they are in different pairs). RAID is not a backup: it does not protect against deletion or corruption.\n(c) Before changing metadata (inodes, bitmaps, directory entries) the file system writes a description of the update to a journal and commits it; only then does it write the real locations (checkpoint). After a crash, the journal is replayed (committed transactions are applied again) or discarded (incomplete ones), so the metadata is consistent within seconds without a full scan (fsck). This is the same write-ahead idea as in database logging.\n","notes":"# Operating Systems: Lecture Notes\n\nAn operating system (OS) manages the hardware of a computer and gives programs a clean, protected and efficient environment to run in. This course covers processes and threads, CPU scheduling, synchronisation and deadlocks, memory management and virtual memory, storage and file systems. Examples use Linux and C. All the C programs below were compiled with gcc on Ubuntu (WSL2) and run; the outputs shown are real. All scheduling, paging, deadlock and disk numbers were computed with scripts. Prerequisites: Computer Organization, Data Structures and Algorithms, and C programming.\n\n# Chapter 1: Introduction\n\n## 1.1 What an OS does\n- **Resource manager**: shares the CPU, memory, disks and devices among programs fairly and efficiently.\n- **Abstraction provider**: hides hardware detail (a file instead of disk blocks, a process instead of raw CPU state, virtual memory instead of physical RAM).\n- **Protector**: one program cannot read or corrupt another or the kernel.\n\n## 1.2 Kernel, user mode and system calls\nThe CPU has at least two modes. In **kernel mode** all instructions and memory are accessible; in **user mode** privileged instructions (I/O, changing page tables, halting) trap. A program asks the kernel for a service through a **system call** (open, read, write, fork, exec, wait, mmap, ...), which executes a trap instruction, switches to kernel mode, runs the kernel handler and returns. **Interrupts** (timer, device) and **exceptions** (page fault, divide by zero) also enter the kernel. The **timer interrupt** lets the OS take the CPU back from a running program (preemption).\n\nKernel structures: **monolithic** (Linux: everything in one privileged image, with loadable modules), **microkernel** (minimal kernel, servers in user space), **hybrid** (Windows, macOS), and **virtual machines / containers**. A container shares the host kernel but isolates processes with namespaces and control groups.\n\n## 1.3 Boot sequence\nFirmware (UEFI) initialises hardware, loads the bootloader from disk, which loads the kernel; the kernel initialises memory management, devices and the first user process (init/systemd, PID 1) which starts the rest.\n\n# Chapter 2: Processes\n\n## 2.1 The process abstraction\nA **process** is a program in execution: an address space (code, data, heap, stack), CPU registers (program counter, stack pointer), open files and other resources. The kernel keeps a **process control block (PCB)** for each: PID, state, saved registers, scheduling information, memory-management information, open-file table.\n\n**States**: new, ready, running, waiting (blocked on I/O or an event), terminated. Transitions: the scheduler dispatches ready to running; a timer interrupt moves running to ready; an I/O request moves running to waiting; I/O completion moves waiting to ready.\n\n**Context switch**: save the registers of the old process in its PCB, load the new process's registers and page table. It is pure overhead (typically microseconds). If a round-robin quantum is 10 ms and a switch costs 2 ms, only 10/12 = 83 percent of the CPU does useful work.\n\n## 2.2 Creating processes on Unix\n`fork()` creates a copy of the calling process. It returns 0 in the child and the child's PID in the parent. The child has its own copy of the address space (implemented lazily with copy-on-write). `exec()` replaces the current program image with a new program. `wait()` lets a parent collect a child's exit status.\n\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n#include <sys/wait.h>\n#include <unistd.h>\nint x = 10;\nint main(void) {\n    int fd[2];\n    pipe(fd);\n    pid_t pid = fork();\n    if (pid == 0) {\n        x += 5;                       /* the child has a private copy of x */\n        close(fd[0]);\n        char msg[64];\n        snprintf(msg, sizeof msg, \"child sees x=%d\", x);\n        write(fd[1], msg, strlen(msg) + 1);\n        close(fd[1]);\n        exit(42);\n    }\n    close(fd[1]);\n    char buf[64];\n    read(fd[0], buf, sizeof buf);\n    int status;\n    waitpid(pid, &status, 0);\n    printf(\"parent got: \\\"%s\\\"; parent x=%d; child exit status=%d\\n\", buf, x, WEXITSTATUS(status));\n    return 0;\n}\n```\nOutput: `parent got: \"child sees x=15\"; parent x=10; child exit status=42`. The child's change to x is invisible to the parent: separate address spaces. The pipe is the communication channel.\n\n**How many processes?** Each `fork()` doubles the number of processes that execute the following code. Three consecutive `fork()` calls give 2^3 = **8** processes (the test program printed exactly 8 lines). A loop `for (i = 0; i < n; i++) fork();` also creates 2^n processes in total.\n\nRunning another program:\n```c\npid_t pid = fork();\nif (pid == 0) {\n    execlp(\"echo\", \"echo\", \"hello from the child program\", (char *)NULL);\n    perror(\"execlp\");          /* reached only if exec fails */\n    _exit(127);\n}\nint status;\nwaitpid(pid, &status, 0);\n```\nOutput: `hello from the child program` and then `child exited with status 0`. This fork-then-exec split is how a shell runs commands: it forks, the child sets up redirection (`dup2`) and execs, the parent waits.\n\n**Pipes in a shell.** `printf \"a\\nb\\nc\\n\" | wc -l` is two processes connected by a pipe; `dup2` makes the pipe the child's stdout or stdin, and the parent must close both pipe ends, otherwise the reader never sees end-of-file. The program printed **3**. The pipe buffer on Linux is 65,536 bytes by default: a writer blocks when it is full.\n\n**Zombies and orphans.** A terminated child stays a **zombie** (state Z, only its PCB entry remains) until the parent calls `wait`. Test: the child exited, the parent slept 1 s and looked at `/proc/<pid>/stat`: state `Z`; after `waitpid` the entry disappeared. An **orphan** (parent died first) is adopted by init, which reaps it.\n\n## 2.3 Signals\nA **signal** is an asynchronous software notification (SIGINT from Ctrl-C, SIGSEGV for a bad memory access, SIGCHLD when a child ends, SIGKILL which cannot be caught, SIGUSR1 for the user). A process installs a handler with `sigaction`. A test program sent SIGUSR1 to itself and its handler ran (signal number 10 on Linux). Handlers must only call async-signal-safe functions and should set a `volatile sig_atomic_t` flag.\n\n## 2.4 Interprocess communication\n- **Pipes** and FIFOs: byte streams. **Message queues**. **Shared memory** (fastest; needs synchronisation). **Sockets**: local or network. **Signals**: notifications only.\n\n# Chapter 3: Threads\n\nA **thread** is a unit of execution inside a process: it has its own stack and registers but shares code, heap, globals and open files with the other threads of the process. Benefits: cheap creation and switching, simple sharing, parallelism on multicore CPUs. Cost: shared memory means **races**.\n\nModels: user-level threads (one kernel entity, fast but one blocking call blocks all), kernel threads (one-to-one, used by Linux and Windows), many-to-many. POSIX threads (pthreads) is the standard C API: `pthread_create`, `pthread_join`, `pthread_mutex_*`, `pthread_cond_*`.\n\n## 3.1 A data race, measured\n```c\nlong counter = 0;\nvoid *work(void *arg) {\n    for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) counter++;\n    return NULL;\n}\n/* two threads each run work(); expected final value 2,000,000 */\n```\n`counter++` compiles to load, add, store; two threads can both load the same value and one increment is lost. The measured results of three runs of the unprotected counter were **1,976,498**, **1,976,669** and **1,971,753** (about 24,000 to 28,000 updates lost, different each time). The same loop protected with a mutex gave 2,000,000 every time, as did an atomic add. A race produces wrong answers that vary from run to run, which is what makes them hard to debug.\n\n# Chapter 4: CPU Scheduling\n\n## 4.1 Concepts and metrics\nThe **short-term scheduler** picks which ready process runs. CPU-bound processes run long bursts, I/O-bound ones short bursts. A scheduler may be **non-preemptive** (a process runs until it blocks or finishes) or **preemptive** (it can be interrupted). Metrics:\n- **Turnaround time** = completion - arrival. **Waiting time** = turnaround - burst. **Response time** = first run - arrival. **Throughput**, **CPU utilisation**, **fairness**.\n\n## 4.2 The algorithms, worked on one example\nProcesses (arrival, burst): P1 (0, 8), P2 (1, 4), P3 (2, 9), P4 (3, 5).\n\n**FCFS** (first come, first served, non-preemptive): order P1, P2, P3, P4. Completion times 8, 12, 21, 26. Waiting times 0, 7, 10, 18: **average 8.75**. Simple, but short jobs wait behind long ones (**convoy effect**).\n\n**SJF** (shortest job first, non-preemptive): at t = 8 the shortest ready is P2 (4), then P4 (5), then P3 (9). Completion: P1 8, P2 12, P4 17, P3 26. Waiting: 0, 7, 15 (P3), 9 (P4): **average 7.75**. Provably minimal average waiting among non-preemptive schedules, but needs the burst length in advance (estimated by exponential averaging: tau_next = a t_last + (1 - a) tau_old) and can starve long jobs.\n\n**SRTF** (shortest remaining time first = preemptive SJF): timeline P1 [0,1], P2 [1,5], P4 [5,10], P1 [10,17], P3 [17,26]. Completion: P2 5, P4 10, P1 17, P3 26. Waiting: P1 9, P2 0, P3 15, P4 2: **average 6.5**.\n\n**Round robin** (preemptive, time quantum q = 3): Gantt chart P1 [0,3], P2 [3,6], P3 [6,9], P4 [9,12], P1 [12,15], P2 [15,16], P3 [16,19], P4 [19,21], P1 [21,23], P3 [23,26]. Completion: P2 16, P4 21, P1 23, P3 26. Waiting: P1 15, P2 11, P3 15, P4 13: **average 13.5**. RR gives good response time and fairness. A very large q degenerates to FCFS; a very small q wastes time on context switches. A rule of thumb: about 80 percent of CPU bursts should be shorter than q.\n\n**Priority scheduling**: run the highest-priority ready process; may starve low-priority processes, fixed by **aging** (raise priority with waiting time). Example, all arriving at 0 (burst, priority; lower number means higher priority): P1 (10, 3), P2 (1, 1), P3 (2, 4), P4 (1, 5), P5 (5, 2). Order P2, P5, P1, P3, P4, completion 1, 6, 16, 18, 19, waiting 0, 1, 6, 16, 18: **average 8.2**.\n\n**Multilevel feedback queue (MLFQ)**: several queues with different priorities and quanta; a process that uses its whole quantum drops a level (CPU-bound), one that blocks early stays high (interactive); periodic boosting prevents starvation. This is the basis of schedulers in Windows, macOS and (as CFS, which uses virtual runtime and a red-black tree) Linux.\n\n**Real-time**: rate-monotonic (shorter period, higher priority) and earliest-deadline-first scheduling (see Real-Time courses). Rate-monotonic is schedulable if utilisation <= n(2^(1/n) - 1) (69.3 percent as n grows).\n\n**Multiprocessor** scheduling: per-CPU run queues, processor affinity, load balancing.\n\n# Chapter 5: Synchronisation\n\n## 5.1 The critical-section problem\nA **critical section** is code that accesses shared data. A solution must provide **mutual exclusion**, **progress** (the decision of who enters cannot be postponed forever by processes outside), and **bounded waiting** (no starvation).\n\n**Peterson's algorithm** (two threads, shared `flag[2]` and `turn`):\n```c\nlock(i): flag[i] = 1; turn = j; while (flag[j] && turn == j) ;   /* spin */\nunlock(i): flag[i] = 0;\n```\nIt satisfies all three conditions, but on modern CPUs it works only with sequentially consistent atomics (plain variables can be reordered by the compiler and CPU). A C11 version using `atomic_int` ran with two threads, one million entries each: counter = **2,000,000**.\n\n**Hardware support**: atomic **test-and-set**, **compare-and-swap**, fetch-and-add; used to build **spinlocks** (busy waiting, good for very short sections on multicore) and the blocking locks below. Disabling interrupts works only on a single CPU kernel.\n\n## 5.2 Mutexes and semaphores\nA **mutex** gives exclusive access: `lock()` blocks if held, `unlock()` wakes a waiter. A **semaphore** is an integer S with atomic operations: `wait(S)` (P): if S > 0 then S = S - 1 else block; `signal(S)` (V): S = S + 1 and wake a waiter. A **binary** semaphore (0 or 1) acts as a lock; a **counting** semaphore counts resources.\n\n**Producer-consumer (bounded buffer)** with semaphores `empty` (initially N) and `full` (initially 0) and a mutex for the buffer:\n```\nproducer:  wait(empty); lock(mu); put(item); unlock(mu); signal(full);\nconsumer:  wait(full);  lock(mu); item = get(); unlock(mu); signal(empty);\n```\nTested in C with an 8-slot buffer and 100,000 items: the sum produced equaled the sum consumed (5,000,050,000 = 100,000 × 100,001/2). **Order matters**: acquiring the mutex before `wait(empty)` can deadlock (a producer holding the mutex waits forever for space while the consumer cannot get the mutex).\n\n**Readers-writers**: many readers or one writer. A POSIX read-write lock (`pthread_rwlock_t`) provides it. Policies can starve writers or readers.\n\n**Dining philosophers**: five philosophers, five forks. If everyone picks up the left fork first, all may hold one fork and wait forever (deadlock). Solutions: **resource ordering** (always pick up the lower-numbered fork first; this breaks circular wait), allow at most four at the table, or an arbiter. The ordered version in C finished 5 × 20,000 meals with no deadlock.\n\n## 5.3 Monitors and condition variables\nA **monitor** bundles shared data, the procedures that access it and implicit mutual exclusion; **condition variables** (`wait`, `signal`/`notify`) let a thread sleep inside a monitor until a condition holds. In pthreads, always re-check the condition in a loop:\n```c\npthread_mutex_lock(&m);\nwhile (!ready) pthread_cond_wait(&c, &m);   /* releases m while sleeping */\n/* use shared data */\npthread_mutex_unlock(&m);\n```\nThe loop is required because of spurious wakeups and because another thread may have changed the condition before this one runs.\n\n## 5.4 Deadlock\nA set of processes is **deadlocked** if each waits for a resource held by another in the set. **Four necessary conditions** (Coffman): (1) mutual exclusion, (2) hold and wait, (3) no preemption, (4) circular wait. Break any one to prevent deadlock.\n\nExperiment (C, two threads, opposite lock orders with a 1-second timed lock to detect it): T1 locks A then wants B; T2 locks B then wants A: both **timed out (deadlock)**. With the consistent order (A then B for both) both got both locks. Resource ordering is the standard fix.\n\n**Resource-allocation graph**: edges request (process to resource) and assignment (resource to process). With single-instance resources a cycle means deadlock; with multiple instances a cycle is necessary but not sufficient.\n\n**Handling**: prevention (attack a condition), avoidance (banker's algorithm), detection and recovery (abort or preempt), or ignore it (the \"ostrich\" approach used by most general-purpose systems).\n\n**Banker's algorithm** (avoidance). Data: Available (a vector), Max, Allocation, Need = Max - Allocation. A state is **safe** if there is an order in which each process can finish with the resources currently free plus those released by the earlier ones. Safety algorithm: Work = Available; repeatedly find a process with Need <= Work, add its Allocation to Work, mark it finished; the state is safe if all finish.\n\nClassic example: 5 processes, 3 resource types with totals (10, 5, 7).\n\n```\n        Allocation   Max       Need\nP0      0 1 0        7 5 3     7 4 3\nP1      2 0 0        3 2 2     1 2 2\nP2      3 0 2        9 0 2     6 0 0\nP3      2 1 1        2 2 2     0 1 1\nP4      0 0 2        4 3 3     4 3 1\nAvailable = (3, 3, 2)\n```\nSafe sequence: Work = (3,3,2). P1 can run (need (1,2,2) <= work): Work = (5,3,2). P3 (need (0,1,1)): Work = (7,4,3). P4 (need (4,3,1)): Work = (7,4,5). P0 (need (7,4,3)): Work = (7,5,5). P2 (need (6,0,0)): Work = (10,5,7). **Safe sequence <P1, P3, P4, P0, P2>**. Resource-request algorithm: P1 requests (1,0,2): it is <= Need and <= Available, so pretend to grant it; the new state is still safe (same sequence), so **grant**. P0 requests (0,2,0) in the original state: the new state is safe too (sequence <P3, P1, P2, P0, P4>), so it could be granted. But **after P1's request has been granted** (Available = (2,3,0)), the same request by P0 would leave Available = (2,1,0) and no process could then finish: the state is **unsafe**, so P0 must wait. Likewise P4's request (3,3,0) at that point exceeds Available and must wait. Whether a request is granted depends on the current state, not only on the request.\n\n**Detection**: the same algorithm using current requests instead of Need; processes that cannot finish are deadlocked. **Recovery**: abort all deadlocked processes, abort one at a time, or preempt resources with rollback.\n\n# Chapter 6: Memory Management\n\n## 6.1 Address spaces\nA program uses **logical (virtual) addresses**; the **memory-management unit (MMU)** translates them to **physical addresses** using page tables. Protection: each process can touch only its own mapped pages.\n\n## 6.2 Contiguous allocation and fragmentation\nVariable partitions with placement policies. Example: free partitions 100, 500, 200, 300, 600 KB; requests 212, 417, 112, 426 KB in order.\n- **First fit** (first partition big enough): 212 goes into 500 (leaving 288), 417 into 600 (leaving 183), 112 into 288 (leaving 176), 426 **cannot be placed**.\n- **Best fit** (smallest sufficient): 212 into 300, 417 into 500, 112 into 200, 426 into 600: **all placed**.\n- **Worst fit** (largest): 212 into 600 (388 left), 417 into 500, 112 into 388, 426 **cannot be placed**.\n**External fragmentation**: enough free memory in total but not contiguous (fixed by compaction or paging). **Internal fragmentation**: waste inside an allocated unit.\n\n## 6.3 Paging\nPhysical memory is divided into **frames** and the logical address space into **pages** of the same size (a power of two, typically 4 KB). The address splits into a **page number** (index into the page table) and an **offset**. The page table entry gives the frame number plus bits: valid, read/write, user/supervisor, accessed (reference), dirty.\n\nExample: 32-bit virtual addresses, 4 KB pages. The offset is 12 bits and the page number 20 bits, so a process has 2^20 = 1,048,576 pages. With 4-byte entries a flat page table is 4 MB per process. Address 0x1A2B3C: page number 0x1A2, offset 0xB3C. If page 0x1A2 is in frame 0x0F7 the physical address is 0x0F7B3C.\n\nInternal fragmentation: a 72,766-byte process with 2048-byte pages needs 36 pages, wasting 2048 - (72,766 mod 2048) = 2048 - 1086 = **962 bytes** in its last page (on average half a page).\n\n**Multilevel page tables** keep only the needed parts: a two-level scheme with 32-bit addresses splits 10 + 10 + 12 bits; each second-level table has 1024 entries × 4 B = 4096 B = exactly one page. x86-64 uses four levels of 9 bits (512 entries × 8 bytes = one 4 KB page per table) plus a 12-bit offset for 48-bit virtual addresses. **Inverted** and hashed page tables index by frame instead.\n\n**TLB** (translation lookaside buffer): a small associative cache of recent translations. **Effective access time** with a hit ratio h, TLB lookup 10 ns and memory access 100 ns: EAT = h (10 + 100) + (1 - h)(10 + 200). For h = 0.98: 0.98 × 110 + 0.02 × 210 = **112 ns**; for h = 0.90: 0.9 × 110 + 0.1 × 210 = **120 ns**.\n\n## 6.4 Virtual memory and demand paging\nPages are loaded only when touched. A reference to a page whose valid bit is clear causes a **page fault**: the OS finds the page on disk, picks a free frame (evicting a victim if necessary), reads the page, updates the table and restarts the instruction. Test: a program touched every page of a 100 MB mapping once: 100 MB / 4096 B = **25,600 pages** and the kernel reported **25,600 minor page faults** (each first touch allocated a zero-filled frame).\n\n**Cost**: with a memory access of 200 ns, a fault service of 8 ms and fault rate p: EAT = (1 - p) × 200 + p × 8,000,000 ns. For p = 1/1000: **8199.8 ns**, a 41 times slowdown. To keep the slowdown below 10 percent (EAT <= 220 ns) p must be below (220 - 200)/(8,000,000 - 200) = **2.5 × 10^-6**: fewer than one fault per 400,000 references.\n\n**Copy-on-write**: after `fork` parent and child share frames marked read-only; the first write to a page faults and the kernel copies it.\n\n## 6.5 Page replacement\nWhen no frame is free the OS evicts a page. Reference string 7, 0, 1, 2, 0, 3, 0, 4, 2, 3, 0, 3, 2, 1, 2, 0, 1, 7, 0, 1 with 3 frames:\n- **FIFO** (evict the oldest): **15 faults**.\n- **LRU** (evict the least recently used): **12 faults**.\n- **OPT** (Belady's optimal, evict the page used farthest in the future; impossible to implement but the benchmark): **9 faults**.\n- **Clock** (second chance, approximates LRU with a reference bit): 14 faults here.\n\nBy number of frames (FIFO, LRU, OPT): 3 frames 15, 12, 9; 4 frames 10, 8, 8; 5 frames 9, 7, 7.\n\n**Belady's anomaly**: with FIFO, more frames can mean more faults. String 1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 5, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5: FIFO with 3 frames = **9** faults but with 4 frames = **10**. LRU and OPT are **stack algorithms** and never show it (LRU: 10 with 3 frames, 8 with 4).\n\n**Thrashing**: if a process has fewer frames than its **working set** (the pages it uses in a recent window), it faults constantly and CPU use collapses. Remedies: working-set or page-fault-frequency allocation; reduce the degree of multiprogramming. **Locality** (temporal and spatial) is what makes paging work at all.\n\n**Other**: global versus local replacement, prepaging, huge pages (2 MB reduce TLB pressure), memory-mapped files, swapping and the OOM killer.\n\n# Chapter 7: Mass Storage and I/O\n\n## 7.1 Disks\nA hard disk's access time = seek + rotational latency + transfer. At 7200 rpm one rotation takes 8.33 ms, so the average rotational latency is **4.17 ms**; with a 9 ms average seek and a 4 KB transfer at 200 MB/s (0.02 ms) a random read takes about **13.2 ms**, which is why sequential access (no seeks) matters. SSDs have no seek time but pages must be erased in blocks (write amplification, wear levelling, TRIM).\n\n## 7.2 Disk scheduling\nCylinders 0 to 199, head at 53, request queue 98, 183, 37, 122, 14, 124, 65, 67:\n- **FCFS**: total head movement **640** cylinders.\n- **SSTF** (shortest seek time first): order 65, 67, 37, 14, 98, 122, 124, 183: **236** (can starve far requests).\n- **SCAN** (elevator): moving toward 0 first: 37, 14, 0, then 65, 67, 98, 122, 124, 183: **236**.\n- **LOOK** (SCAN that turns back at the last request): moving down first: **208**; moving up first: 299.\n- **C-SCAN** moves in one direction and jumps back, giving more uniform waiting.\nModern SSDs and NVMe devices do their own scheduling; the OS uses simple schedulers (for example none or mq-deadline in Linux).\n\n## 7.3 RAID\n**RAID 0** striping (speed, no redundancy); **RAID 1** mirroring; **RAID 5** striping with distributed parity (n disks give n - 1 of capacity and survive one failure); **RAID 6** two parity blocks (survive two failures); **RAID 10** striped mirrors. Example: four 1 TB disks: RAID 0 = 4 TB (no fault tolerance), RAID 5 = 3 TB, RAID 10 = 2 TB. RAID is not a backup.\n\n## 7.4 I/O mechanisms\nProgrammed I/O (polling), **interrupt-driven I/O**, and **DMA** (the controller transfers data to memory without the CPU). Drivers hide device differences behind a uniform interface; buffering and caching (the page cache) hide latency.\n\n# Chapter 8: File Systems\n\n## 8.1 Concepts\nA **file** is a named sequence of bytes with attributes (size, owner, permissions, times). A **directory** maps names to files; hierarchical namespace; **hard links** (several names for one inode) and **symbolic links** (a file holding a path). Unix permissions: read, write, execute for owner, group, others (for example 0644 = rw-r--r--). The file API: open, read, write, lseek, close; the kernel keeps a per-process file-descriptor table pointing into the system-wide open-file table.\n\n## 8.2 Allocation methods\n- **Contiguous**: fast, but external fragmentation and growth problems.\n- **Linked** (FAT): each block holds the next block number, kept in a table (the file allocation table in memory). FAT32 with 4 KB clusters on a 1 TB volume has 2^40/2^12 = 268,435,456 entries = **1 GiB** of table at 4 bytes each: it is large.\n- **Indexed / inode-based** (Unix ext4, with extents): the inode holds attributes and block pointers: 12 direct pointers, 1 single-indirect, 1 double-indirect, 1 triple-indirect. With 4 KB blocks and 4-byte pointers a block holds 1024 pointers. Maximum file size = (12 + 1024 + 1024^2 + 1024^3) × 4 KB: direct 48 KiB, single indirect 4 MiB, double indirect 4 GiB, triple indirect 4 TiB, total about **4.004 TiB (4100.004 GiB)**. A 5 MiB file needs 1280 blocks: 12 direct, so 1268 through indirect blocks (the single indirect covers 1024, the rest through the double indirect).\n\n**Free-space management**: bitmaps or free lists. **Journaling** (ext4, NTFS): write intended metadata changes to a log before applying them, so after a crash the file system replays or discards the log instead of scanning the whole disk; same idea as write-ahead logging in databases. **Copy-on-write file systems** (ZFS, Btrfs) never overwrite live data and add snapshots and checksums.\n\n## 8.3 Performance\nThe **page cache** keeps recently used file blocks in RAM; read-ahead prefetches sequentially; writes are buffered and flushed later (`fsync` forces them to disk). Sequential access avoids seeks.\n\n# Chapter 9: Protection, Security and Virtualisation (overview)\n- **Access control**: ACLs and capabilities; the principle of least privilege; users, groups, root.\n- **Common attacks**: buffer overflow (mitigations: stack canaries, ASLR, non-executable stacks), privilege escalation, race conditions in file access (TOCTOU), malware.\n- **Authentication**: salted password hashes, multi-factor.\n- **Virtualisation**: a hypervisor runs several guest OSs using hardware support (VT-x, AMD-V) and nested page tables; **containers** (Docker) isolate processes with namespaces and cgroups but share the kernel and are lighter than VMs.\n\n## Quick reference\n- Waiting time = turnaround - burst; turnaround = completion - arrival.\n- Four deadlock conditions: mutual exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption, circular wait.\n- Page number = address / page size; offset = address mod page size. Pages for n address bits and a p-bit offset: 2^(n - p).\n- EAT (TLB) = h (t_tlb + t_mem) + (1 - h)(t_tlb + 2 t_mem). EAT (faults) = (1 - p) t_mem + p t_fault.\n- Disk access = seek + rotational latency (half a rotation on average) + transfer.\n","plan":"# Operating Systems: 14-Week Study Plan and Resources\n\nTime budget: 9 to 11 hours per week (3 reading, 6 to 7 programming and problem solving, 1 review with the app's quizzes). You need a Linux environment: install Ubuntu (a virtual machine, dual boot, or WSL2 on Windows) with gcc, make and gdb. Operating systems are learned by writing programs that use the kernel's services and by breaking them (races, deadlocks, leaks) on purpose.\n\n## Textbooks\n- Remzi and Andrea Arpaci-Dusseau, *Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces* (OSTEP). Free online, the clearest modern text; the chapter on each topic matches this course.\n- Abraham Silberschatz, Peter Galvin and Greg Gagne, *Operating System Concepts* (the \"dinosaur book\"). The standard university text.\n- Andrew Tanenbaum and Herbert Bos, *Modern Operating Systems*.\n- Michael Kerrisk, *The Linux Programming Interface*. The reference for system calls.\n- Randal Bryant and David O'Hallaron, *Computer Systems: A Programmer's Perspective*.\n\n## Free courses and tools\n- MIT 6.S081 Operating System Engineering (xv6 labs, free videos and code); MIT 6.1810 labs.\n- OSTEP homework simulators (scheduler, paging, disk, RAID simulators are free Python programs).\n- Tools: gcc, gdb, `strace` (watch system calls), `ltrace`, `valgrind` and `-fsanitize=thread` (ThreadSanitizer finds data races), `perf`, `/proc` and `ps`, `top`, `vmstat`, `iostat`.\n- Optional: build and boot xv6 or write a small kernel in QEMU.\n\n## Week by week\n\n**Week 1: Introduction, system calls, kernel and user mode (Ch 1).** Do: Assignment 1 Q1. Use `strace ls` and count the system calls; write a \"hello world\" using only `write`.\n\n**Week 2: Processes: fork, exec, wait, pipes, signals (Ch 2).** Do: Assignment 1 Q2, Q7. Write a tiny shell that runs commands and supports one pipe.\n\n**Week 3: Threads and data races (Ch 3).** Do: Assignment 1 Q3. Reproduce the lost-update counter and fix it with a mutex, an atomic and per-thread counters; time the three versions.\n\n**Week 4: CPU scheduling (Ch 4).** Do: Assignment 1 Q4, Q5, Q6. Sit the **Class Test** at the end of the week (Chapters 1 to 4 plus a first look at synchronisation).\n\n**Week 5: Critical sections, locks, atomic instructions (Ch 5.1).** Do: Assignment 2 Q1. Implement a spinlock with compare-and-swap and compare it to a pthread mutex.\n\n**Week 6: Semaphores, producer-consumer, readers-writers (Ch 5.2).** Do: Assignment 2 Q2, Q3. Write the bounded buffer with semaphores and with condition variables; test with several producers and consumers.\n\n**Week 7: Monitors, dining philosophers, classic bugs (Ch 5.3).** Do: Assignment 2 Q4. Mock midterm: first half of the Final Exam.\n\n**Week 8: Deadlock (Ch 5.4).** Do: Assignment 2 Q5, Q6, Q7. Write a program that deadlocks, then fix it with lock ordering; run ThreadSanitizer.\n\n**Week 9: Memory management: contiguous allocation, paging, TLBs (Ch 6.1 to 6.3).** Do: Assignment 3 Q1, Q2, Q3, Q5. Write a simulator that translates virtual to physical addresses with a two-level page table.\n\n**Week 10: Virtual memory and page replacement (Ch 6.4, 6.5).** Do: Assignment 3 Q4. Write FIFO, LRU and OPT simulators and check them against the notes (15, 12, 9 faults); watch real faults with `/usr/bin/time -v`.\n\n**Week 11: Mass storage, disk scheduling, RAID, I/O (Ch 7).** Do: Assignment 3 Q6. Implement SSTF, SCAN and C-SCAN and compare total head movement.\n\n**Week 12: File systems (Ch 8).** Do: Assignment 3 Q7. Explore inodes with `stat`, hard and symbolic links; write a program that lists a directory with `opendir` and `stat`.\n\n**Week 13: Security, virtualisation, project (Ch 9).** Project: write a shell with pipes and redirection, or a user-level thread library, or a small file system on a file image, with a design report.\n\n**Week 14: Revision.** Redo missed Class Test and assignment questions, then sit the **Final Exam** under exam conditions (3 hours, formula sheet only).\n\n## Milestone checklist\n- [ ] I can write programs with fork, exec, wait, pipes and signals and predict their output.\n- [ ] I can explain a context switch and the process states.\n- [ ] I can compute average waiting time for FCFS, SJF, SRTF, RR and priority scheduling and draw Gantt charts.\n- [ ] I can identify data races and fix them with mutexes, semaphores and condition variables.\n- [ ] I can solve producer-consumer, readers-writers and dining-philosophers problems.\n- [ ] I can apply the four deadlock conditions, resource-allocation graphs and the banker's algorithm.\n- [ ] I can translate addresses with single and multilevel page tables and compute TLB and page-fault effective access times.\n- [ ] I can simulate FIFO, LRU and OPT replacement and explain Belady's anomaly and thrashing.\n- [ ] I can compute disk scheduling distances and explain RAID levels.\n- [ ] I can explain inodes, directories, links, allocation methods and journaling.\n- [ ] I scored at least 70 percent on the Final Exam with a formula sheet only.\n\n## Study habits that work\n1. Run every example from the notes and change it: break it, measure it, explain the output.\n2. Draw the state of the system (queues, frames, tables, graphs) on paper for every algorithm.\n3. When code misbehaves under concurrency, assume a race first; use ThreadSanitizer and `strace`.\n4. Keep a table of formulas (EAT, page-table size, disk access time, banker's checks) and derive them rather than memorising.\n5. Read real kernel code in small pieces (xv6 is about 10,000 lines).\n","test":"# Operating Systems: Class Test\n\nDuration: 60 minutes. Calculator allowed. Total: 60 marks. Section A: 20 multiple-choice questions (1 mark each). Section B: 4 problems (10 marks each).\n\n## Section A: Multiple choice (20 marks)\n\n**1.** Which of these is NOT a standard process state?\nA) ready  B) running  C) compiling  D) waiting\n\n**2.** In the child process, `fork()` returns\nA) 0  B) the child's PID  C) -1  D) the parent's PID\n\n**3.** A zombie process is one that\nA) is running very slowly  B) has terminated but has not yet been waited for by its parent  C) has lost its parent  D) is blocked on I/O\n\n**4.** Threads of the same process share\nA) their stacks  B) their registers  C) the heap and global variables  D) their program counters\n\n**5.** Which of these is a non-preemptive scheduling algorithm?\nA) round robin  B) SRTF  C) FCFS  D) multilevel feedback queue\n\n**6.** Which non-preemptive algorithm gives the smallest average waiting time?\nA) FCFS  B) round robin  C) priority  D) SJF\n\n**7.** The convoy effect occurs in\nA) FCFS  B) round robin  C) SRTF  D) multilevel queues\n\n**8.** Aging is used to prevent\nA) deadlock  B) starvation  C) thrashing  D) fragmentation\n\n**9.** Which of these is NOT a requirement of a critical-section solution?\nA) mutual exclusion  B) progress  C) bounded waiting  D) maximum parallelism\n\n**10.** A semaphore initialised to 1 is normally used as\nA) a counter of free slots  B) a barrier  C) a mutual-exclusion lock  D) a condition variable\n\n**11.** A call to `pthread_cond_wait` should be placed\nA) inside a while loop that re-checks the condition  B) inside an if statement  C) outside the mutex  D) in a signal handler\n\n**12.** Which is NOT one of the four Coffman conditions for deadlock?\nA) mutual exclusion  B) hold and wait  C) circular wait  D) preemption of resources\n\n**13.** The banker's algorithm is a method of deadlock\nA) prevention  B) avoidance  C) detection  D) recovery\n\n**14.** A safe state is one in which\nA) deadlock is certain  B) there is an order in which all processes can finish  C) no resources are allocated  D) all processes are waiting\n\n**15.** With 4 KB pages the offset of a virtual address has\nA) 10 bits  B) 11 bits  C) 12 bits  D) 13 bits\n\n**16.** A TLB is\nA) a disk cache  B) a type of page table stored on disk  C) a CPU register  D) a cache of recent page-table translations\n\n**17.** Belady's anomaly can occur with\nA) FIFO replacement  B) LRU replacement  C) OPT replacement  D) all of these\n\n**18.** Thrashing happens when\nA) the disk is full  B) processes spend most of their time paging because they lack frames for their working sets  C) the CPU overheats  D) too many files are open\n\n**19.** Which disk scheduling algorithm can starve requests?\nA) FCFS  B) SCAN  C) SSTF  D) C-SCAN\n\n**20.** A Unix inode contains\nA) the file name  B) the directory path  C) the file data only  D) the file attributes and block pointers\n\n## Section B: Problems (40 marks)\n\n**B1. Scheduling (10 marks)** Processes (arrival, burst): P1 (0, 5), P2 (1, 3), P3 (2, 8), P4 (3, 6). Draw Gantt charts and compute the waiting time of each process and the average for (a) FCFS, (b) non-preemptive SJF, (c) SRTF, (d) round robin with q = 3 (a process arriving at the same instant that another is preempted joins the queue first) [10].\n\n**B2. Synchronisation (10 marks)** (a) Two threads share `int balance = 100`. T1 executes `balance += 50` and T2 executes `balance -= 30`, each compiled to load, modify, store. List every possible final value and give an interleaving for each [4]. (b) Using semaphores, force statement S1 in thread T1 to run before statement S2 in thread T2 [2]. (c) A bounded buffer has 5 slots. Give the initial values of the semaphores `empty`, `full` and the mutex [2]. (d) Why is a spinlock wasteful on a single CPU? [2]\n\n**B3. Deadlock (10 marks)** Four processes and three resource types. Available = (1, 1, 2). Allocation: P0 (1,0,1), P1 (2,1,0), P2 (0,1,1), P3 (1,1,0). Max: P0 (3,2,2), P1 (4,2,1), P2 (1,2,2), P3 (3,2,1). (a) Compute Need [2]. (b) Is the state safe? Show the safety algorithm [5]. (c) Does an unsafe state mean the system is deadlocked? [1] (d) State the four conditions for deadlock and which one resource ordering breaks [2].\n\n**B4. Memory (10 marks)** (a) A system has 4 KB pages and a page table maps page 0x1A to frame 0x3C. Translate the virtual address 0x1A6F3 [3]. (b) A machine has 32-bit virtual addresses and 8 KB pages with 4-byte page-table entries: the number of pages and the size of a flat page table [3]. (c) Reference string 2, 3, 2, 1, 5, 2, 4, 5, 3, 2, 5, 2 with 3 frames: the number of faults for FIFO and LRU [4].\n\n---\n\n## Answer Key and Solutions\n\n### Section A\n1 **C** · 2 **A** · 3 **B** · 4 **C** · 5 **C** · 6 **D** · 7 **A** · 8 **B** · 9 **D** · 10 **C** · 11 **A** · 12 **D** · 13 **B** · 14 **B** · 15 **C** · 16 **D** · 17 **A** · 18 **B** · 19 **C** · 20 **D**\n\nBrief reasons: 3. The parent has not called wait; only the PCB entry remains. 7. Short jobs wait behind a long one. 12. The condition is \"no preemption\", not preemption. 14. A safe state has a safe sequence; an unsafe state may or may not lead to deadlock. 17. FIFO is not a stack algorithm. 19. SSTF keeps serving nearby requests and far ones may wait indefinitely. 20. The file name is stored in the directory entry, not the inode.\n\n### Section B\n\n**B1.**\n(a) FCFS: P1 [0,5], P2 [5,8], P3 [8,16], P4 [16,22]. Waiting: P1 0, P2 4, P3 6, P4 13: **average 5.75**.\n(b) SJF: P1 [0,5]; at 5 the ready jobs are P2 (3), P3 (8), P4 (6): P2 [5,8], P4 [8,14], P3 [14,22]. Waiting: P1 0, P2 4, P3 12, P4 5: **average 5.25**.\n(c) SRTF: P1 [0,1]; P2 arrives at 1 with burst 3 < 4 remaining: P2 [1,4]; at 4 the ready jobs are P1 (4), P3 (8), P4 (6): P1 [4,8], P4 [8,14], P3 [14,22]. Completion: P2 4, P1 8, P4 14, P3 22. Waiting: P1 3, P2 0, P3 12, P4 5: **average 5.0**.\n(d) RR q = 3: P1 [0,3], P2 [3,6], P3 [6,9], P4 [9,12], P1 [12,14], P3 [14,17], P4 [17,20], P3 [20,22]. Completion: P2 6, P1 14, P4 20, P3 22. Waiting: P1 9, P2 2, P3 12, P4 11: **average 8.5**.\n\n**B2.**\n(a) Correct serial results give 100 + 50 - 30 = **120**. If both load 100 and T1 stores last: **150**; if T2 stores last: **70**. Interleavings: T1 loads 100; T2 loads 100; T2 stores 70; T1 stores 150 gives 150. T1 loads 100; T2 loads 100; T1 stores 150; T2 stores 70 gives 70. All three values {120, 150, 70} are possible.\n(b) A semaphore `sync` initialised to 0. T1: S1; `signal(sync)`. T2: `wait(sync)`; S2.\n(c) `empty` = 5 (free slots), `full` = 0 (filled slots), `mutex` = 1.\n(d) The thread that spins consumes its whole time slice waiting for a lock that is held by a thread that cannot run (it is not scheduled while the spinner has the only CPU). The lock holder only makes progress when the spinner is preempted. A blocking lock would sleep immediately.\n\n**B3.**\n(a) Need = Max - Allocation: P0 (2,2,1), P1 (2,1,1), P2 (1,1,1), P3 (2,1,1).\n(b) Step 1: Work = Available = (1,1,2).\nStep 2: P0 needs (2,2,1): 2 > 1, no.\nStep 3: P1 needs (2,1,1): 2 > 1, no.\nStep 4: P2 needs (1,1,1) <= (1,1,2): yes.\nStep 5: P2 finishes: Work = (1,1,2) + (0,1,1) = (1,2,3).\nStep 6: P0 (2,2,1): 2 > 1, no.\nStep 7: P1 (2,1,1): no.\nStep 8: P3 (2,1,1): no.\nStep 9: No other process can finish: there is no safe sequence.\nStep 10: The state is **unsafe**.\n(c) **No**: unsafe means deadlock is possible, not certain (processes may not use their maximum claims at the same time). The banker's algorithm refuses to enter unsafe states.\n(d) Mutual exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption, circular wait. Resource ordering breaks **circular wait**.\n\n**B4.**\n(a) Step 1: 4 KB pages: offset is 12 bits (3 hex digits). 0x1A6F3: page number = 0x1A6F3 >> 12 = **0x1A**, offset = **0x6F3**.\nStep 2: frame 0x3C: physical address = (0x3C << 12) + 0x6F3 = 0x3C000 + 0x6F3 = **0x3C6F3**.\n(b) Step 1: 8 KB pages: offset 13 bits, page number 32 - 13 = 19 bits.\nStep 2: 2^19 = **524,288 pages**; the table = 524,288 × 4 B = **2 MB**.\n(c) FIFO: 2 F, 3 F, 2 hit, 1 F, 5 F (evict 2), 2 F (evict 3), 4 F (evict 1), 5 hit, 3 F (evict 5), 2 hit, 5 F (evict 2), 2 F (evict 4): **9 faults**. LRU: 2 F, 3 F, 2 hit, 1 F, 5 F (evict 3), 2 hit, 4 F (evict 1), 5 hit, 3 F (evict 2), 2 F (evict 4), 5 hit, 2 hit: **7 faults**. (OPT gives 6.)\n"},mcq:[{"q":"Which of these is NOT a standard process state?","options":["ready","running","compiling","waiting"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.025,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"In the child process, `fork()` returns","options":["0","the child's PID","-1","the parent's PID"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.075,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A zombie process is one that","options":["is running very slowly","has terminated but has not yet been waited for by its parent","has lost its parent","is blocked on I/O"],"answer":1,"explain":"The parent has not called wait; only the PCB entry remains.","f":0.125,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Threads of the same process share","options":["their stacks","their registers","the heap and global variables","their program counters"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.175,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Which of these is a non-preemptive scheduling algorithm?","options":["round robin","SRTF","FCFS","multilevel feedback queue"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.225,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Which non-preemptive algorithm gives the smallest average waiting time?","options":["FCFS","round robin","priority","SJF"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.275,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The convoy effect occurs in","options":["FCFS","round robin","SRTF","multilevel queues"],"answer":0,"explain":"Short jobs wait behind a long one.","f":0.325,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Aging is used to prevent","options":["deadlock","starvation","thrashing","fragmentation"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.375,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Which of these is NOT a requirement of a critical-section solution?","options":["mutual exclusion","progress","bounded waiting","maximum parallelism"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.425,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A semaphore initialised to 1 is normally used as","options":["a counter of free slots","a barrier","a mutual-exclusion lock","a condition variable"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.475,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A call to `pthread_cond_wait` should be placed","options":["inside a while loop that re-checks the condition","inside an if statement","outside the mutex","in a signal handler"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.525,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Which is NOT one of the four Coffman conditions for deadlock?","options":["mutual exclusion","hold and wait","circular wait","preemption of resources"],"answer":3,"explain":"The condition is \"no preemption\", not preemption.","f":0.575,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"The banker's algorithm is a method of deadlock","options":["prevention","avoidance","detection","recovery"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.625,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A safe state is one in which","options":["deadlock is certain","there is an order in which all processes can finish","no resources are allocated","all processes are waiting"],"answer":1,"explain":"A safe state has a safe sequence; an unsafe state may or may not lead to deadlock.","f":0.675,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"With 4 KB pages the offset of a virtual address has","options":["10 bits","11 bits","12 bits","13 bits"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.725,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A TLB is","options":["a disk cache","a type of page table stored on disk","a CPU register","a cache of recent page-table translations"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.775,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Belady's anomaly can occur with","options":["FIFO replacement","LRU replacement","OPT replacement","all of these"],"answer":0,"explain":"FIFO is not a stack algorithm.","f":0.825,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Thrashing happens when","options":["the disk is full","processes spend most of their time paging because they lack frames for their working sets","the CPU overheats","too many files are open"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.875,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"Which disk scheduling algorithm can starve requests?","options":["FCFS","SCAN","SSTF","C-SCAN"],"answer":2,"explain":"SSTF keeps serving nearby requests and far ones may wait indefinitely.","f":0.925,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A Unix inode contains","options":["the file name","the directory path","the file data only","the file attributes and block pointers"],"answer":3,"explain":"The file name is stored in the directory entry, not the inode.","f":0.975,"src":"Class test"},{"q":"A system call causes the CPU to","options":["stay in user mode","switch to kernel mode through a trap","reboot","flush the TLB only"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.025,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"After `fork()` in Linux, parent and child initially","options":["share all memory writable","have separate address spaces (copy-on-write)","share the stack only","have no open files"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.075,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"`exec()` does what?","options":["creates a new process","replaces the current program image","waits for a child","kills the parent"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.125,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A pipe's reader sees end-of-file only when","options":["the writer sleeps","the buffer is empty","all write ends are closed","the reader closes its end"],"answer":2,"explain":"The write end may also be held open by another process, e.g. the parent, which is why it must close it.","f":0.175,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Which signal cannot be caught or ignored?","options":["SIGINT","SIGUSR1","SIGCHLD","SIGKILL"],"answer":3,"explain":"The correct answer is D.","f":0.225,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The effect of a very large round-robin quantum is that RR behaves like","options":["SJF","FCFS","priority scheduling","SRTF"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.275,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"SRTF is","options":["preemptive SJF","non-preemptive FCFS","round robin","a real-time algorithm"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.325,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In a multilevel feedback queue, a process that uses its entire quantum is typically","options":["moved up to a higher priority","moved down to a lower priority","terminated","swapped out"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.375,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A race condition exists when","options":["two threads run on different CPUs","the result depends on the relative timing of unsynchronised accesses to shared data","a thread sleeps","a mutex is locked twice"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.425,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A counting semaphore initialised to N is used to","options":["provide mutual exclusion only","count N identical resources","measure time","wake all threads"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.475,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In the producer-consumer solution with semaphores, calling `lock(mutex)` before `wait(empty)` can cause","options":["starvation only","deadlock","a race","no problem"],"answer":1,"explain":"A producer holding the mutex can wait for space that only a consumer (needing the mutex) can create.","f":0.525,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Dining philosophers with each philosopher picking up the left fork first can","options":["deadlock","never deadlock","livelock only","starve only the first philosopher"],"answer":0,"explain":"The correct answer is A.","f":0.575,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Breaking the circular-wait condition can be done by","options":["allowing preemption","imposing a total order on resources","allocating all resources at once","using more memory"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.625,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In a resource-allocation graph with multiple instances per resource, a cycle","options":["always means deadlock","is necessary but not sufficient for deadlock","never means deadlock","means a safe state"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.675,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"The page size is 2 KB and the address 5000 lies in page number","options":["1","2","3","5"],"answer":1,"explain":"5000/2048 = 2.44, so page 2.","f":0.725,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A multilevel page table saves memory because it","options":["compresses pages","allocates tables only for the parts of the address space in use","removes the TLB","uses larger pages"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.775,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A page fault occurs when","options":["the TLB hits","a referenced page is not in memory (valid bit clear)","the disk fails","a process ends"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.825,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"Which page replacement algorithm is optimal but unimplementable?","options":["FIFO","LRU","OPT","Clock"],"answer":2,"explain":"The correct answer is C.","f":0.875,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"In RAID 5 with n disks, usable capacity is","options":["n","n - 1","n / 2","n - 2"],"answer":1,"explain":"One disk's worth of parity.","f":0.925,"src":"Final exam"},{"q":"A hard link","options":["is a file containing a path name","is a second directory entry referring to the same inode","cannot be deleted","always crosses file systems"],"answer":1,"explain":"The correct answer is B.","f":0.975,"src":"Final exam"}]});
